import Foundation

/// Independently authored directions for the traditional no-cut turtle.
enum TurtleLessonContent {
    static let isComplete = true
    static let material = "One 15 × 15 cm square · No cutting or glue"
    static let sourceName = "Origami.me · Traditional Turtle"
    static let sourceURL = URL(string: "https://origami.me/turtle/")!
    static let steps: [LessonStep] = [
        ("Prepare your square", "Place a 15 cm square with an edge facing you. Start with the white side facing up. Keep a little space around the paper for the four legs.", "ONE SQUARE · WHITE SIDE UP", "The head, shell, tail and all four legs come from this one square. This model uses folds and gentle shaping, without cuts or glue."),
        ("Find the vertical middle", "Bring the left edge across to the right. Press the center fold and reopen the square.", "MATCH THE SIDE EDGES · OPEN AGAIN", "The center line provides a consistent target for the two bottom corners."),
        ("Bring in the first corner", "Bring the near-left corner to the center of the square. Match the sloping fold before pressing.", "LEFT BOTTOM CORNER TO CENTER", "This triangular layer stays attached to the sloping edge and later contributes to the upper flaps."),
        ("Match the second corner", "Bring the near-right corner to the same center point. Leave the two corners folded.", "RIGHT BOTTOM CORNER TO CENTER", "The two sloping lower edges should meet evenly at the point nearest you."),
        ("Fold the first sloping edge", "Move the left lower sloping edge toward the middle line. Let its attached corner layer travel with it; do not add another crease across that loose layer.", "SLOPING EDGE TO THE MIDDLE", "A loose layer can cross a crease in your view without belonging to the part being creased. Watch which paper edge is actually attached."),
        ("Fold the matching edge", "Fold the right sloping edge to the middle in the same way. Turn the model so the diamond-shaped flap points away from you.", "MATCH THE OTHER EDGE · TURN THE MODEL", "Turning the paper changes the viewing direction without adding a fold. The lower flaps are now accessible."),
        ("Raise the first lower flap", "Bring the inner corner of the left lower flap to the bottom point of the diamond. Fold every layer of this small corner together.", "LOWER LEFT CORNER UP TO THE POINT", "This diagonal is the first half of a pleat that will shorten the rear leg."),
        ("Fold that flap outward", "Fold the raised flap back outward along the nearby diagonal. Leave a narrow strip between the two creases.", "MAKE THE SECOND PART OF THE PLEAT", "Two nearby folds turn the flap back toward the outside while shortening its reach. The exact narrow strip is a shaping choice."),
        ("Raise the other lower flap", "Bring the matching inner corner on the right up to the bottom point of the diamond.", "LOWER RIGHT CORNER UP TO THE POINT", "Keep this crease balanced with the one on the left."),
        ("Match the outward pleat", "Fold the right flap outward again. Compare the two shortened flaps and adjust them gently before pressing.", "MATCH THE TWO REAR-LEG FLAPS", "Both flaps come from the original sheet. Neither leg is added as a separate piece."),
        ("Lower the upper point", "Fold the top section down across the wide horizontal guide. Bring the upper point toward the lower point of the diamond.", "TOP SECTION FOLDS DOWN", "The layers connected through this section must travel together. Folding only the visible surface would separate connected paper."),
        ("Raise the head section", "Fold the pointed section back up along the second horizontal guide. Leave a narrow band behind the raised point.", "LEAVE A BAND BEHIND THE HEAD", "This second horizontal fold leaves a band behind the raised point. The next part of the full design reshapes that band and the neighboring layers together.")
        ,("Prepare the first head-side fold", "On the right, bring the short lower edge of the band toward its upper edge. Press the diagonal and open it again.", "PRECREASE THE RIGHT SIDE · REOPEN", "This guide lets the band pivot while the neighboring head layer changes shape."),
        ("Swivel the first head side", "Lift the right side of the head. Press the band along the diagonal you just made, allowing the small inner triangle to turn under. Flatten the raised pocket gently.", "LIFT THE HEAD EDGE · CLOSE THE SMALL POCKET", "Three connected sections move together. Let the pocket open during the movement instead of forcing the paper flat too early."),
        ("Prepare the other head side", "Repeat the short edge-to-edge precrease on the left side of the band, then reopen it.", "PRECREASE THE LEFT SIDE · REOPEN", "Matching the two sides gives the head an even width and keeps both front-leg flaps accessible."),
        ("Match the second swivel", "Lift the left head edge and close its small pocket in the same way. Compare the two sides before pressing the head flat.", "MATCH THE HEAD · KEEP BOTH FRONT FLAPS FREE", "The finished head sits between two pointed flaps. Those flaps will become the front legs."),
        ("Pinch the tail dart", "Pinch the narrow fold running from the lower shell toward the tail. Bring its neighboring crease inward and let the shell rise. Turn the turtle shell-side up.", "PINCH THE TAIL PLEAT · LET THE SHELL RISE", "Closing a small dart gives the broad shell some volume. The shell can flex while you adjust it; it does not need to become a sharp pyramid."),
        ("Round the first shell corner", "Loosen the tail pleat enough to reach the first side corner. Tuck that small shell corner underneath, leaving the nearby leg free. Restore the shell's curve.", "TUCK ONLY THE SHELL CORNER UNDER", "A small hidden corner softens the outline. The loose leg may overlap this area in your view, so check that it stays outside."),
        ("Round the matching corner", "Tuck the opposite shell corner underneath by a similar amount. Re-form the tail pleat and compare the shell edges.", "MATCH THE TWO SHELL EDGES", "These small corner folds are shaping choices. Use the model as a proportion guide rather than measuring a new exact landmark."),
        ("Lock the tail pleat", "Ease the shell open slightly. Fold the very end of the tail inward, then return a shorter point outward. Close the shell dart again.", "TAIL IN · SHORTER POINT BACK OUT", "This tiny return pleat holds the tail fold and leaves a visible point behind the shell."),
        ("Shape the first front leg", "Turn a narrow strip of the first pointed front leg underneath. Begin near the free tip and ease the bend toward its root.", "NARROW THE FREE EDGE OF THE FRONT LEG", "A tapered bend adds a little depth to the leg. Keep the root relaxed so the shell and head retain their shape."),
        ("Shape the other front leg", "Narrow the matching edge of the other front leg. Compare the two tips from above.", "MATCH THE TWO FRONT LEGS", "Small differences are normal when shaping paper. Adjust the bend until both front legs look balanced."),
        ("Curl the first rear foot", "Bend the triangular end of the first rear leg back underneath. Keep the broad root of the leg outside the shell.", "TURN THE REAR FOOT UNDER", "The returned tip gives this foot a shorter, rounded outline and helps the turtle rest on a surface."),
        ("Finish the matching rear foot", "Turn the other rear tip underneath to match. Rest the turtle on the table and gently balance all four legs.", "MATCH THE REAR FEET · TURTLE COMPLETE", "Look from above and from the side: you should see the head, four separate legs, a short tail and a raised shell. Gentle final shaping makes each paper turtle individual.")
    ].enumerated().map { index, row in
        LessonStep(id: index, title: row.0, instruction: row.1, cue: row.2, explanation: row.3)
    }
